    "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-revisions": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-revisions",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Drift, Reconciliation, and Integration Outcomes"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-revisions-p": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-revisions-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Reconciliation updates target identity from each new build report. When its explicit rename rules establish continuity, it moves facts, evidence, and links to the successor in the same transaction. It does not attach claims to a guessed replacement. Facts on retired or unresolved targets remain stored."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-drift-facts-p": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-drift-facts-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The deterministic drift flagger rechecks cited code spans against the checkout head. It compares the current span digest with the cited digest and reports unchanged, drifted, or unresolvable evidence. The librarian receives that report when revisiting the subject."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "lifecycle-b-evidence-drift-gate": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-evidence-drift-gate",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The consumer gate then re-runs the flagger after apply: clean completes the pass; drift left on the first attempt releases the task for one bounded retry; drift left after the retry completes with the warning recorded in the pass artifact. See "
        },
        {
          "insert": "Drift Gate",
          "attributes": {
            "reference": {
              "kind": "doc",
              "path": "10-system-design/20-knowledge-base/30-knowledge-updates/10-librarian-processing",
              "label": "Drift Gate"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "lifecycle-b-evidence-drift-edits": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-evidence-drift-edits",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The flag never silently edits a fact. A drop or a re-cite uses an ordinary librarian overwrite, replacing the fact's evidence rows wholesale, or a clear, deleting the fact and its evidence rows together. Drift itself never deletes knowledge: facts on a retired or unresolved target stay stored."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-regressions-p": {
      "id": "lifecycle-b-sks-lifecycle-regressions-p",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "An integration conflict is recorded on "
        },
        {
          "insert": "worker_run.integration",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "integration_detail",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". It does not change a fact merely because a score or merge outcome changed. The librarian revises the claim when its meaning or support changes, and can cite the failed attempt through "
        },
        {
          "insert": "attempt://",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
